the new omicron variant of the  coronavirus is leading to fresh worries  that another round of the pandemic may  be starting it's also drawing attention  to the unequal distribution of vaccines  around the world and the issue of  vaccine patents  scientists first discovered the new  variant in south africa a country where  just over 40 of the population is fully  vaccinated  a recent study showed the world's  wealthiest nations about 16 of the  global population have secured the vast  majority of vaccine doses  and that accounts for over 70 percent of  the five leading vaccines in 2021  and that's led to disparities such as  this 60 of people across the european  union are vaccinated compare that to a  mere 3.4 percent across the african  continent  more than 100 countries have agreed to a  petition before the world trade  organization to have vaccine patents  temporarily waived  the eu and germany however are opposed  to that proposal  now for more on this i'm joined by janet  minon he's a visiting senior fellow with  the aseas yusof ishaq institute in  singapore jayden it's good to have you  on the show there was supposed to be a  wto meeting on this topic in part on  this topic today it was cancelled  because of fears of that variant  to what extent is this vaccine patent  issue still a burning one or is it one  that's been more or less decided given  the opposition by some groups in some  countries  i think the  the vaccine  waiver on the  intellectual property rights  will not really make it this time even  without the meeting because the wto  works on a consensus rule and as long as  one country holds out it doesn't go  through and as you just mentioned  uh the eu has already made it very clear  that they won't be voting for the  temporary waiver  and so there's really little prospect of  it happening  so i think they have to look at  what they can do in the  immediate term uh in dealing with the  vaccine inequities  why has the eu been opposed to this  proposal to waive vaccine patents  i think the answer is quite clear i mean  there's a large number of  pharmaceutical companies that would not  be benefiting if such a waiver were to  go through  and on the other side a lot of  companies in india and south africa  would also benefit should it happen but  the problem is not  a long-term one at the moment it's  immediate access and even this way we  wouldn't have overcome that problem we  need to get the current supplies by the  current manufacturers  more equitably distributed more quickly  that's the pressing need for dealing  with the inequity and not the long-term  problem having more production in  different countries  will the new variant add more pressure  to get that done or will such efforts  falter on the same fault lines that they  have to this point  i think the new variant is put a  spotlight on the issue  i don't know if it will help solve the  problem directly it won't affect the  waiver  but you know now in some countries  vaccine hesitancy is playing its role in  limiting  uptake so i think the new variant may  reduce that we saw delta reducing that  hesitancy  but there's also all kinds of logistics  issues that need to be overcome in poor  countries you know there's cold chain  requirements even regular refrigeration  uh of vaccines is difficult in remote  parts of poor countries so i in short i  don't think it will change things very  much there are other things that need to  be done  all right jay menon is a visiting senior  fellow with the isis yusuf ishaq  institute in singapore thank you very  much  thank you also  you
 

